{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Every man by his standard, with signs.</b> From the expression (Jeremiah 10:2) אותות השמים — 'the signs of the heavens'; these are signs that were on every standard. On the standard of Reuben was the figure of a man, alluding to the mandrakes (דודאים); on the standard of Judah was the figure of a lion, for so did his father liken him to a young lion; on the standard of Ephraim was the figure of an ox, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:17) בכור שורו הדר לו; and on the standard of Dan was the figure of an eagle, so that they resembled the cherubim that Ezekiel saw." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret אותות ('with signs') from the expression (Deuteronomy 12:21) בכל אות נפשך — 'with all the desire of your soul'; for so Israel desired the standards at the holy assembly when they saw there the standards of the ministering angels. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Song of Songs 5:10) דודי צח ואדום דגול מרבבה, and the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in Shir HaShirim Rabbah that the Holy One, blessed be He, came to Sinai with the ministering angels arranged in standards. And they further expounded there (Song of Songs 2:4) הביאני אל בית היין — 'He brought me to the house of wine' — this is Mount Sinai, on which the Torah was given, and it is likened to wine; and ודגלו עלי אהבה — 'his standard over me was love' — this is the Shekhinah. And so Scripture explained (Proverbs 15:17) טוב ארוחת ירק ואהבה שם — 'better is a meal of herbs where love is' — by way of the Kabbalah. Therefore Israel desired that standards should be upon them like theirs." "<b>And thus</b> was the arrangement of the standards according to what we find in the Scriptures. You already know that the twelve tribes were divided into four standards, three to every direction of the four directions of the world, just as the twelve supernal tribes, known above, are divided into four camps, three to every direction, and they are called the four camps of the Shekhinah. And each tribe had a cloth-banner, one for each and every tribe, and the banners differed in their hues and colors, distinguished one from another, in the manner of the supernal standards seen at Sinai, which were like the form of flames of fire in many hues and colors. Judah, Issachar, and Zebulun, who were in the east: Judah's banner was the color of the likeness of the sky, painted upon it a lion; Issachar's banner was similar to blue, painted upon it the sun and the moon, because they were expert in the solstices and constellations; Zebulun's banner was white, a sign of his wealth, for silver is white, and painted upon it a ship. Reuben, Simeon, and Gad, who were in the south: Reuben's banner was red, painted upon it mandrakes; Simeon's banner was green, painted upon it the city of Shechem; Gad's banner was mixed white and black, painted upon it a raiding band. Ephraim, Manasseh, and Benjamin, who were in the west: Ephraim and Manasseh's banners were the color of the appearance of shoham stone; on Ephraim's was painted an ox, and on Manasseh's a wild ox; Benjamin's banner included all the colors, painted upon it a wolf. Dan, Asher, and Naphtali, who were in the north: Dan's banner was similar to sapphire, painted upon it a serpent; Asher's banner was similar to the light of oil, painted upon it an olive tree; Naphtali's banner was similar to clear wine, painted upon it a doe. And now understand well, and I shall arrange them all by their names — the supernal tribes of YH and the lower ones." "<b>The standard of the camp of Judah.</b> It journeyed first and was in the east. And because he possessed valor, he was likened to a lion, as it is said (Genesis 49:9) גור אריה יהודה. With him was the tribe of Issachar and the tribe of Zebulun, and concerning them it is written ראשונה יסעו — 'they shall journey first.' And this word alludes to wisdom (חכמה), for all of them were masters of Torah, as it is said (Psalms 60:9) יהודה מחוקקי, and it is written (I Chronicles 12:33) ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים, and it is written (Judges 5:14) ומזבולן מושכים בשבט ספר. Therefore they are fit to dwell together, and all three are called the camp of Judah. And they were the counterpart of the camp of Gavriel, Azriel, Shemaiel before the Shekhinah, and all three are called the camp of Gavriel. And each of them has many camps and many hosts of angels under their authority, just as in the tribe of Issachar and the tribe of Zebulun there were many thousands and myriads of Israel. From this, understand the forceful allusion that Scripture hinted when it says (I Chronicles 5:2) יהודה גבר באחיו — it comes to allude that the camp of Judah is the counterpart of the camp of Gavriel." "<b>The standard of the camp of Reuben.</b> It was in the south, and he was a master of repentance; and repentance is a great kindness from the Creator, blessed be He, upon His creatures. And he first opened with rescue and mercy, as it is said (Genesis 37:21) וישמע ראובן ויצילהו. With him was the tribe of Simeon, who was born after him, and the tribe of Gad, who is the firstborn of Leah's maidservant. And concerning them it is written ושניים יסעו — 'the second they shall journey' — for so understanding (בינה) is second to wisdom (חכמה). And all three are called the camp of Reuben. And they were the counterpart of the camp of Michael, Kokhaviel, Padiel, to the right of the Shekhinah, and all three are called the camp of Michael. This is what Moses our master, peace be upon him, alluded to when he opened the Song at the Sea (Exodus 15:11) מי כמוכה, and concluded in the Torah (Deuteronomy 33:26) אין כאל ישרון — behold Michael (מי כמוכה / אין כאל). And you should know that he is the angel of kindness and mercy. And after this it is written: ונסע אהל מועד מחנה הלוים בתוך המחנות — 'and the tent of meeting, the camp of the Levites, shall journey in the midst of the camps.' And you should know that the camp of the Levites was twenty-two thousand, as it is said (Numbers 3:39) כל זכר מבן חדש ומעלה שנים ועשרים אלף — counterpart to the angels who descended at Sinai, who were twenty-two thousand, as it is said (Psalms 68:18) רכב אלהים רבותים אלפי שנאן. And these twenty-two thousand were near the Shekhinah." "<b>And behold,</b> the Levites, ministers of the Sanctuary, merited this supernal rank, for their number was like the number of the ministering angels who are near the Shekhinah. And there is no doubt that there descended there many hosts and mighty camps of angels besides these, and they were far from the Shekhinah because their rank was not so great. And likewise the mustered ones of Israel were far from the Ark, in the manner written מנגד, which is an expression of distance, as in the phrase (Genesis 21:16): ״ותשב לה מנגד הרחק״ — 'and she sat over against him, far off.'" "<b>And you should know</b> that just as the camp of Israel was four banners for the four winds of the world surrounding the camp of the Levites — as it says, מחנה הלוים בתוך המחנות, meaning that the camp of the Levites was in the middle, within the four camps of Israel — so the camp of the Levites surrounded the Tent of Meeting for the four winds of the world, for Moses, Aaron, and his sons were on the east, and the sons of Gershon on the west, and the sons of Kohath on the south, and the sons of Merari on the north, and the Tent of Meeting was in the middle, within the Levites. And what Scripture says, ונסע אהל מועד מחנה הלוים בתוך המחנות, means thus: the Tent of Meeting traveled within the camp of the Levites, and the camp of the Levites within the camps of Israel; so the middlemost point belonged to the Tent of Meeting, with the four camps of Israel surrounding it מנגד. And this corresponds to the Shekhinah, whose four camps surround Her; and this is what it says in the passage of the Chariot: 'and the Shekhinah of El, exalted and uplifted, is in the middle, and His throne is high and lofty above.'" "<b>The banner of the camp of Ephraim, by their hosts, westward.</b> You know that Jeroboam sinned and caused the many to sin, and he was of the tribe of Ephraim; therefore he needed healing to mend his fracture. With him were the tribe of Manasseh and the tribe of Benjamin, and this is what is written (Psalms 80:3): ״לפני אפרים ובנימין ומנשה עוררה את גבורתך״ — 'before Ephraim and Benjamin and Manasseh, stir up Your might.' And they encamped in the west, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the Shekhinah is in the west. And of them it is written: ושליעים יסעו. And it is fitting to draw near to Torah and to repentance mightily (גבורה), so that a person may return in repentance and prevail over his inclination. And all three of them are called the camp of Ephraim, and it was in the pattern of the camp of Raphael — Zavdiel, Ahaziel — behind the Shekhinah, and all three are called the camp of Raphael. This is what Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, alluded to regarding Miriam, who needed healing, and said (Numbers 12:13): ״אל נא רפא נא לה״ — 'El, please heal her, please' — behold, Raphael." "<b>The banner of the camp of Dan, northward.</b> It is well known that the tribe of Dan accepted the two golden calves that Jeroboam made, as it is written (1 Kings 12:29): ״את האחד נתן בבית אל ואת האחד נתן בדן״ — 'he placed one in Bethel and placed one in Dan.' And it was fitting that he be in the north, for from there wealth goes out into the world, and Dan darkened all the world with idolatry. And Scripture says (Isaiah 29:15): ״והיה במחשך מעשיהם״ — 'and their deeds are in the darkness.' For the east — the sun emerges from there; also the south — the sun travels there in its height; therefore it is called דרום, which is דר רום, meaning 'dwelling of height,' that is, it dwells in the height of the firmament. Also the west — since the sun sets there, it walks in that direction. But in the north wind the sun never shines; therefore it is called צפון, because the sun is hidden (צפון) from there, and all is darkness. And this is the direction fitting for the tribe of Dan, who darkened the world with idolatry. Therefore he needed atonement, that the Holy One, blessed be He, would atone for him and shine upon him, in the manner written (Micah 7:8): ״כי אשב בחושך ה' אור לי״ — 'though I sit in darkness, HaShem is my light.' And with him were the tribe of Asher and the tribe of Naphtali, to shine upon his darkness — from what is written (Deuteronomy 33:24): ״וטובל בשמן רגלו״ — 'and he dips his foot in oil,' and oil is called light; and of Naphtali it is written (Deuteronomy 33:23): ״נפתלי שבע רצון ומלא ברכת ה'״ — 'Naphtali, sated with favor and full of the blessing of HaShem.' And of them it is written: לאחרונה יסעו. And according to the pattern in the other banners, it should have said ורביעים יסעו, but it said לאחרונה, to allude to the foundation stone, the tenth, the final Heh. And all three of them are called the camp of Dan, and they were in the pattern of the camp of Uriel — Daniel, Rumael — to the left of the Shekhinah, and all three are called the camp of Uriel. This is what King David, peace be upon him, alluded to regarding the atonement of the offerings (Psalms 118:27): ״אל ה' ויאר לנו״ — 'El is HaShem, and He has shone upon us' — behold, Uriel." "<b>And know</b> and understand that just as the arrangement of the four banners in the desert was in the pattern of the four camps of the Shekhinah, so the arrangement itself is among the four holy living creatures (חיות הקדש). And thus is the arrangement for all: the living creature whose name is Lion (אריה), and the camp of Gabriel, and the banner of the camp of Judah, to the east; the living creature whose name is Ox (שור), and the camp of Raphael, and the banner of the camp of Ephraim, to the west; the living creature whose name is Man (אדם), and the camp of Michael, and the banner of the camp of Reuben, to the south; the living creature whose name is Eagle (נשר), and the camp of Uriel, and the banner of the camp of Dan, to the north. And understand well what I have mentioned, and you will grasp the power of our holy Torah, and how the hosts of Israel below are bound and held fast to the hosts of the ministering angels above — and then the mysteries of wisdom will be revealed to you. And in order to demonstrate that the twelve tribes of Jacob correspond to the twelve ministering angels of the tribes above, these that I have mentioned, all of which are sealed with the name El, you will find the names of the princes of the tribes who stand at the four winds also sealed with the name El." "<b>And behold,</b> this too is a wondrous matter, and it would have been fitting to conceal it. For the prince who stood on the east side, under the banner of Judah, his name was Netanel; and the prince who stood on the south side, under the banner of Reuben, his name was Shelumiel; and the one who stood on the west side, under the banner of Ephraim, his name was Gamliel; and the one who stood on the north side, under the banner of Dan, his name was Pagiel. And you will not find among the rest of the names that any one of them ends with the name El besides these four who stood under the four banners at the four winds of the world. And so you will find this stated explicitly in the section of the princes' offerings, which they brought in their order according to the arrangement of the banners: on the first day, Nahshon son of Amminadav, for the tribe of Judah; on the second day, Netanel son of Zuar, prince of Issachar; on the third day, prince of the children of Zebulun, Eliav son of Helon; on the fourth day, prince of the children of Reuben, Elitzur son of Shedeur; on the fifth day, prince of the children of Simeon, Shelumiel son of Zurishaddai; on the sixth day, prince of the children of Gad, Eliasaf son of Deuel; on the seventh day, prince of the children of Ephraim, Elishama son of Ammihud; on the eighth day, prince of the children of Manasseh, Gamliel son of Pedahzur; on the ninth day, prince of the children of Benjamin, Avidan son of Gideoni; on the tenth day, prince of the children of Dan, Ahiezer son of Ammishaddai; on the eleventh day, prince of the children of Asher, Pagiel son of Ochran; on the twelfth day, prince of the children of Naphtali, Ahira son of Enan. Here you have the twelve princes of the tribes and how they brought their offerings in their order, exactly according to the arrangement that was in the banners. And if you sum the four days on which the four princes whose names end in El brought their offerings — on the second day, when Netanel brought; and on the fifth day, Shelumiel; and on the eighth day, Gamliel; and on the eleventh day, Pagiel — you will find from the reckoning of the second and the fifth and the eighth and the eleventh the number of the special Name, like the reckoning of (Ezekiel 1:1) ״ויהי בשלשים שנה ברביעי בחמשה לחדש״ — which totals 39, and that is the Name of Gevurah, as you know." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״איש על דגלו באותות״ — with the signs that their father handed down to them. When our father Jacob passed away, he commanded his sons that no Egyptian nor any of his grandsons should touch his bier, because among the grandsons were some who had married Canaanite women. And so it says (Genesis 50:12–13): ״ויעשו בניו לו כן כאשר צום״ and ״וישאו אותו בניו ארצה כנען״ — and not 'his grandsons.' He said to them: Judah, Issachar, and Zebulun shall carry from the east; Reuben, Simeon, and Gad shall bear from the south; Ephraim, Manasseh, and Benjamin from the west; Dan, Asher, and Naphtali from the north. Joseph shall not bear, because he is a king. Levi shall not bear, because he bears the Ark of HaShem; and one who bears the Ark of the Living Being of all worlds — it is not fitting that he should bear the bier of a dead man. If you bear my bier according to my command, the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to cause you to dwell by banners in the wilderness. Now Moses was concerned about the arrangement of the banners, lest discord arise among the tribes — who would be in the east, who in the west. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: You have no need to trouble yourself over this, for this matter was already a tradition in their hands from their father." "<b>The children of Israel shall encamp opposite.</b> At a distance of a mil, in the manner of what is said in Joshua (Joshua 3:4): ״אך רחוק יהיה ביניכם וביניו כאלפים אמה במדה״ — by measure, so that they would be able to come there on the Sabbath to pray. And the banners were equally distributed to each direction, and Moses and Aaron were stationed adjacent to the Ark, as it says (Numbers 3:38): ״והחונים לפני המשכן קדמה לפני אהל מועד מזרחה משה ואהרן ובניו.״ The Holy One, blessed be He, said: If I am angry with the children, these will make reconciliation between Me and them. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 20:6): ״נרננה בישועתך״ — this is the salvation at the sea; ״ובשם אלהינו נדגול״ — these are the banners of the wilderness; ״ימלא ה׳ כל משאלותיך״ — with special love did He cherish them by making them banners, so that the children of Reuben would be recognized on their own, and the children of Simeon on their own. And as it is written (Song of Songs 2:4): ״הביאני אל בית היין ודגלו עלי אהבה.״ To what may the matter be compared? To a rich man who had a storehouse full of wine. He went in to inspect it and found it all had turned to vinegar. As he was about to leave the storehouse, he found there one barrel of good wine. He said: This barrel is dearer to me than the entire storehouse. So, too, the Holy One, blessed be He, has seventy nations, and from all of them He found gratification only in Israel. יין in gematria is 70, corresponding to the seventy nations, and over all of them (Song of Songs 2:4): ״ודגלו עלי אהבה.״ And it says (Song of Songs 6:8): ״ששים המה מלכות״ — and they are the chieftains of the children of Seir; ״ושמונים פילגשים״ — Noah and his sons; ״ועלמות אין מספר״ — the children of Ishmael; ״אחת היא יונתי תמתי״ — she who did not intermingle among the nations of the world. Rabbi Yehudah says: ״הביאני אל בית היין״ — to a great wine cellar; this is Sinai, from which Torah went forth, which is expounded in forty-nine faces of pure and forty-nine faces of impure. This is what Scripture says (Song of Songs 6:10): ״מי זאת הנשקפה כמו שחר יפה כלבנה ברה כחמה איומה כנדגלות״ — teaching that the nations would see them, awesome in their banners, and would say to them: ״שובי שובי השולמית״ — return, return, O Shulamite; cleave to the Shekhinah! ״שובי שובי ונחזה בך״ — let us make of you governors and prefects, as it says (Exodus 18:21): ״ואתה תחזה מכל העם,״ which is an expression of authority. And Israel replies: ״מה תחזו בשולמית״ — what authority would you give us? ״כמחולת המחנים״ — can you do for us that grandeur which the Cloud had hovering over us? <b>And those who encamp on the east, toward the sunrise,</b> and he goes on to count: Judah, Issachar, and Zebulun. And they were stationed adjacent to Moses, Aaron, and his sons, for he likewise says further on (Numbers 3:38): ״והחונים לפני המשכן קדמה לפני אהל מועד מזרחה משה אהרן ובניו.״ And because they were near Moses and Aaron, they became great in Torah, as it says (Genesis 49:10): ״לא יסור שבט מיהודה ומחוקק מבין רגליו,״ and likewise Issachar (1 Chronicles 12:33): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים,״ and likewise Zebulun (Judges 5:14): ״ומזבולן מושכים בשבט סופר.״ And because all three were neighbors of the Torah, they all became children of Torah; this is what they said: טוב לצדיק טוב לשכנו. The children of Kehat were in the south, and adjacent to them were Reuben, Simeon, and Gad; and all three were neighbors of Korah and his congregation, and they were destroyed in the schism of Korah; this is what they said: אוי לרשע אוי לשכנו. And there were seven clouds of glory surrounding them: four from the four winds, and one that went before them a journey of three days, and the cloud of the Shekhinah that was among them, and one that spread over them, as it says (Numbers 10:34): ״וענן ה' עליהם יומם בנסעם מן המחנה.״ The cloud that went before them would kill the snakes and scorpions and level the road for them, lowering what was high and raising what was low. And the one that spread over them — so that they should not suffer heat and cold for forty years. And concerning that generation, Solomon, peace be upon him, said in the holy spirit (Song of Songs 6:4): ״יפה את רעיתי כתרצה״ — for I am reconciled to them, and I establish them and seat them. And Moses our Master, peace be upon him, said (Deuteronomy 32:10): ״ימצאהו בארץ מדבר יסובבנהו״ — that He brought them to understanding the ways of Torah; ״יצרנהו כאישון עינו״ — He made them banners like the angels, as it says (Song of Songs 5:10): ״דגול מרבבה,״ and it is written (Psalms 68:18): ״רכב אלהים רבותים אלפי שנאן.״ Thus far in the Midrash."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {8 "The printed text reads ושליעים in comment 2 and ושלישים would be expected per Numbers 2:24; the reading follows the source as given. Comment 3: לאחרונה per Numbers 2:31 where ורביעים would parallel the other banners; translated as written. The phrase 'the foundation stone, the tenth, the final Heh' is rendered hyper-literally; the Kabbalistic allusion is left veiled per the translation register. Angel names in comment 3 (דניאל רמאל) follow the source as printed.", 12 "Source text cites Song of Songs 2:4 for the prooftext 'הביאני אל בית היין ודגלו עלי אהבה', which is the correct verse; however, his manuscript or edition apparently also indicated 5:10–11 and 2:4 together as a combined citation block — reproduced here as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:54.777965603Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}